## Sync your Git Repository

At the beginning of each practical, make sure to sync any changes in the main copy of online repository from GitHub.

1. Open RStudio
2. Choose `File` > `Open Project`
3. Navigate to your **`{{< meta course-title >}}_Students_{{< meta intake >}}`** folder and then to the directory for **`{{< meta practical >}}`**, then load **`{{< meta practical >}}.Rproj`**.
4. In a web browser, log in to GitHub and navigate to your fork of the MB1A_Students repository.
5. Click on `Sync Fork`. If it shows "This branch is not behind the upstream" there is nothing else you need to do.
7. Otherwise, click `Update Branch`. **Do not** click `Discard commits`.
8. Go to your RStudio session and open the `Git` tab.
9. Click `Pull` to update your local copy of the repository.

:::



In this part of the practical, you will need one Quarto document:

* `prac6_exercises.qmd` for basic exercises.

::: {.callout-important}

### Levels

In this practical, the [**core**]{.core-text} material will introduce you to using R for a range of statistical tasks. There is a lot of content, so please focus first on completing the [**core**]{.core-text} material and exercises and reading the [**recommended**]{.rec-text} material, completing the recommended exercises where possible.

If you still have time, the [**extension**]{.extension-text} exercises explore statistical concepts in more depth. There are **a lot** of these — there is no need to complete them all. Choose topics that interest you or that you would like to understand in more depth. The programming may be slightly more advanced, but should be possible using only the R skills introduced in Practicals 1 to 5.

The [**bonus**]{.bonus-text} exercises are intended for students who are already very comfortable with both R and statistics and have plenty of time to spare.

:::

## Writing functions {#sec-writing_functions .core}

[**Core**]{.core-text}

[↑ top](#)

In the practical course so far, we have used many of the different **functions** (see @sec-functions) which are built into R and provided by additional packages.

However, it is also possible to **write your own functions**. This means you can use the same set of instructions multiple times, possibly with different inputs.

Functions have several advantages:

* Using the same code again saves time.
* If you want to change something in the code, you only have to change it once.
* Because you are writing less code, you are less likely to make mistakes.

Here is a simple example of a function, to calculate the equivalent age of a dog if it were a human, its age in *dog years*. According to [the BBC](https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/magazine-22479412), for a medium dog the calculation is:

$$
d = 21 + \left(4 \times (\text{h} - 2)\right)
$$

Where *d* is the age in dog years and *h* is age in human years.

We can write a function to calculate this. We define a function using the function `function()`. It is best to give the function a descriptive name.

The steps of the function are then written in curly brackets (`{}`) on the following lines.

The `return` statement defines what the **output** of the function will be.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
human_years_to_dog_years <- function(age_in_human_years) {
  age_in_dog_years <- 21 + (4 * (age_in_human_years - 2))
  return(age_in_dog_years)
}
```
:::


So here we are saying:

* <code>[human_years_to_dog_years <- function(age_in_human_years)]{.orange-text}</code>: I am defining a function, it is called `human_years_to_dog_years` and takes one argument, `age_in_human_years`.
* <code>[{]{.orange-text}</code>: Here are the steps.
* <code>[age_in_dog_years <- 21 + (4 * (age_in_human_years - 2))]{.orange-text}</code>: Run this equation on the variable `age_in_human_years` and store the result as the variable `age_in_dog_years`.
* <code>[return (age_in_dog_years)]{.orange-text}</code>: Return the value of the variable `age_in_dog_years` as the output of the function
* <code>[}]{.orange-text}</code>: That's all.

Let's use our function to calculate the age in dog years of some dogs.

Monty is 6 in human years.

![Monty](images/monty.jpg){#fig-monty width="25%"}


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
print(human_years_to_dog_years(6))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 37
```


:::
:::



Noodle is 2 in human years:

![Noodle](images/noodle.jpg){#fig-noodle width="25%"}


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
print(human_years_to_dog_years(2))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 21
```


:::
:::


We can also apply our function to a column in a data frame.

Let's say we have this data frame of dogs.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
dog_data <- tibble(
  Name = c(
    "Biscuit",
    "Snowball",
    "Pickle",
    "Artemis",
    "Bert"
  ),
  Age_Human_Years = c(6, 1, 12, 4, 15)
)
gt(dog_data)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}

