### Returning multiple outputs from a function {#sec-functions_multiple_outputs .fold .extension}

So far, our functions have returned a single object. Sometimes we want a function to return several different results.

We can do this by putting the results into a **list**. A list can contain multiple objects, including objects of different types, and we can give each element a name.

For example, this function returns a mean, a vector of centred values, and a data frame:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
calculate_stats <- function(x) {
  x_mean <- mean(x)
  centred_values <- x - x_mean

  summary_df <- tibble(
    observation = x,
    centred = centred_values
  )

  results <- list(
    mean = x_mean,
    centred_values = centred_values,
    summary_data = summary_df
  )

  return(results)
}
```
:::


We can run the function and store the returned list:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
my_results <- calculate_stats(c(2, 4, 6, 8, 10))
print(my_results)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
$mean
[1] 6

$centred_values
[1] -4 -2  0  2  4

$summary_data
# A tibble: 5 × 2
  observation centred
        <dbl>   <dbl>
1           2      -4
2           4      -2
3           6       0
4           8       2
5          10       4
```


:::
:::


We can access an individual named element of the list using $:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
print(my_results$mean)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 6
```


:::

```{.r .cell-code}
print(my_results$centred_values)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] -4 -2  0  2  4
```


:::

```{.r .cell-code}
gt(my_results$summary_data)
```

